{
 "title": "Gradient Descent, intuitively",
 "slides": [
  {
   "id": "landscape",
   "title": "Lost in the fog",
   "kp_ids": [
    "gd1"
   ],
   "idea": "Training a model means finding the lowest point of a loss landscape that we can only feel locally.",
   "check": "Why can't training just look at the whole landscape and jump to the bottom?",
   "objects": [
    {
     "kind": "axes",
     "id": "ax",
     "x": 180,
     "y": 170,
     "w": 1240,
     "h": 560,
     "x_range": [
      -3,
      3
     ],
     "y_range": [
      0,
      6
     ],
     "x_label": "θ  (a setting of the model)",
     "y_label": "L(θ)  how wrong"
    },
    {
     "kind": "curve",
     "id": "loss",
     "axes": "ax",
     "expr": "0.4*(x-0.5)^2+0.5",
     "color": "blue",
     "label": "loss"
    },
    {
     "kind": "dot",
     "id": "you",
     "axes": "ax",
     "curve": "loss",
     "x": -2.3,
     "color": "gold",
     "label": "you are here"
    },
    {
     "kind": "dot",
     "id": "goal",
     "axes": "ax",
     "curve": "loss",
     "x": 0.5,
     "color": "green",
     "label": "lowest loss"
    },
    {
     "kind": "text",
     "id": "fog",
     "text": "You can't see the valley — you can only feel the slope under your feet.",
     "x": 800,
     "y": 850,
     "size": 32,
     "color": "grey"
    }
   ],
   "steps": [
    {
     "narration": "Picture a landscape: sideways is a setting of the model, height is how wrong it is.",
     "actions": [
      {
       "do": "show",
       "ids": [
        "ax",
        "loss"
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "narration": "Training starts somewhere random, usually high up, here.",
     "actions": [
      {
       "do": "show",
       "ids": [
        "you"
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "narration": "We want the bottom of the valley, where the loss is smallest.",
     "actions": [
      {
       "do": "show",
       "ids": [
        "goal"
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "narration": "But with millions of parameters it's foggy: all we can feel is the slope where we stand.",
